🚀 Week 4 – Day 1 Final Keyword & Object Class in Java ☕

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Introduction

Java provides:

✔ `final` keyword to restrict modifications
✔ `Object` class as the root class of all Java classes

👉 These concepts are important for writing secure and reusable programs.

---

🔒 1. final Keyword

The `final` keyword is used to make:

* Variables constant
* Methods non-overridable
* Classes non-inheritable

---

🔹 final Variable

A final variable cannot be changed after initialization.

```java id="f1"
class Demo {
    final int MAX = 100;

    void show() {
        System.out.println(MAX);
    }
}
```

---

❌ Invalid Modification

```java id="f2"
MAX = 200;
```

👉 Causes compilation error.

---

🔹 final Method

A final method cannot be overridden.

```java id="f3"
class Parent {
    final void display() {
        System.out.println("Final Method");
    }
}
```

---

❌ Invalid Override

```java id="f4"
class Child extends Parent {
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Override");
    }
}
```

---

🔹 final Class

A final class cannot be inherited.

```java id="f5"
final class Vehicle {
}
```

---

❌ Invalid Inheritance

```java id="f6"
class Car extends Vehicle {
}
```

---

🎯 Uses of final Keyword

✔ Create constants
✔ Prevent method overriding
✔ Improve security and immutability

---

🌍 2. Object Class

`Object` is the parent class of all Java classes.

```text id="o1"
Every class in Java implicitly extends Object class
```

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Common Methods of Object Class

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 toString()

Returns string representation of object.

```java id="o2"
class Student {
    int id = 101;

    public String toString() {
        return "Student ID: " + id;
    }
}
```

---

🔹 equals()

Compares objects.

```java id="o3"
String a = "Java";
String b = "Java";

System.out.println(a.equals(b));
```

---

🔹 hashCode()

Returns hash value of object.

```java id="o4"
System.out.println(a.hashCode());
```

---

🔹 getClass()

Returns runtime class information.

```java id="o5"
System.out.println(a.getClass());
```

---

🔹 clone()

Creates copy of object.

```java id="o6"
Object copy = obj.clone();
```

---

💻 Complete Example

```java id="o7"
class Student {
    int id;

    Student(int id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    public String toString() {
        return "Student ID: " + id;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student(101);

        System.out.println(s);
        System.out.println(s.getClass());
    }
}
```

